header=read('header-typechecks.json') if (RUN/'header-typechecks.json').exists() else []

content=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-theme="light"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>TDN v3.3 | Blind CSfC audit comparison</title><style>{css}</style></head><body><div class="layout"><nav class="nav"><div class="brand">TDN v3.3</div><p>Revised experiment<br>12 November 2026</p><a href="#results">Results</a><a href="#design">Inputs and controls</a><a href="#repairs">Network repairs</a><a href="#lean">Actual Lean checks</a><a href="#detectors">Detector behavior</a><a href="#requirements">Requirement coverage</a><a href="#cases">Individual cases</a><a href="#limits">Interpretation and limits</a><a href="#records">Records and replay</a><button id="theme">Dark theme</button></nav><main>
<header><div class="eyebrow">Independent document discovery and snapshot proof validation</div><h1>Blind CSfC audits and Lean proof checks</h1><p class="intro">Two fresh Detectors discovered requirements from the original PDF and inspected the networks. TDN applied existing general proofs to fresh snapshots. No Detector received questions, selected requirements, fault counts, or previous results.</p><p>Five networks contain 100 intended requirement violations. One source-reachability case remains ambiguous; its effect on the scores is shown explicitly.</p></header>
<section id="results"><h2>The agents found more of the planted configurations than the instantiated proof suite covered</h2><div class="stats">{''.join(cards)}</div>
<p><b>Exact target matches</b> require the specified requirement ID and affected device. <b>Fault identification</b> also credits a correct description of the same implanted change under another valid requirement. Dark bars show exact matches; lighter bars add related findings.</p>
<p>Astra omitted PF-3 as an explicit finding, but identified all five corresponding GRE allowances under PF-11. Sol identified those allowances and the Inner GRE allowances under PF-11, and the unlisted-peer allowances under PF-20. Those distinctions explain much of the gap between the two scores.</p>
<div class="note sensitivity"><p><b>One intended case needs a stronger ground-truth justification.</b> The net-04 anti-spoofing test proves that the selected source is accepted. The redundant topology prevents the current evidence from conclusively establishing that the source is illegitimate on that interface. Astra explicitly withheld the claim for that reason.</p><p>Removing that case leaves <b>99 supported targets</b>. Fault identification becomes <b>Astra 99/99</b>, <b>Sol 95/99</b>, and <b>TDN 54/99</b>. Exact matches become <b>94/99</b>, <b>80/99</b>, and <b>30/99</b>. The withheld finding should not be presented as an established reasoning error.</p></div>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Astra High</th><th>Sol High</th><th>TDN applications</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Exact requirement recall, nominal 100 cases</th><td>94%</td><td>80%</td><td>30%</td></tr><tr><th>Planted configurations identified, including related findings</th><td>99%</td><td>95%</td><td>55%</td></tr><tr><th>Submitted requirement claims</th><td>104</td><td>85 unique claims in 90 findings</td><td>Lean errors require a separate requirement mapping</td></tr><tr><th>Supported additional requirements</th><td>10 split-tunneling claims</td><td>5 Gray default-denial claims</td><td>Related errors are counted separately</td></tr><tr><th>Reviewed claim precision</th><td>104/104, within each claim’s stated scope</td><td>85/85, within each claim’s stated scope</td><td>65/65 isolated propositions evaluate false; that is not requirement precision</td></tr><tr><th>Agent elapsed time</th><td>{stats['astra']['runtime_seconds']/60:.2f} minutes</td><td>{stats['sol']['runtime_seconds']/60:.2f} minutes</td><td>See the separated collection and checking times below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="footnote">Ordinary accuracy and specificity require true-negative cases. The Detectors saw only faulty deployments, so those measures are unavailable. The clean controls validate the planted behavior and Lean checks; they were not additional Detector trials.</p></section>
<section id="design"><h2>The document and read-only network access were the Detectors’ only inputs</h2><div class="flow"><div><b>1. Prepare the networks</b><span>Restore controls, repair configuration issues, plant the revised faults, and verify actual device behavior.</span></div><div><b>2. Freeze the experiment</b><span>Retain profiles, raw observations, source hashes, prompts, and separate access logs.</span></div><div><b>3. Run independently</b><span>Fresh Astra High and Sol High audits; separate TDN import and Lean proof attempts.</span></div><div><b>4. Evaluate the outputs</b><span>Match evidence-backed findings and failed proof conditions to the private fault records.</span></div></div>
<p>Both agents started without conversation history. Each received a private working directory containing the same 66-page PDF and gateway access details. The prompts asked for an independent audit and a structured evidence-backed response. They supplied no property checklist or target number of findings.</p>
<p>The gateway exposed device inventory, live public configurations, interfaces, routes, filters, IPsec policies and SAs, public certificates and CRLs, process/socket state, FRR/OVS observations, interface settings, and HTTP service content. Private keys, arbitrary host files, mutation scripts, and other deployments were excluded.</p>
<p>The gateway enforced an allowlist of read-only commands. Filesystem restrictions relied on instructions and retrospective inspection of recorded tool calls and scripts. The agents were <b>not isolated by an operating-system sandbox</b>. The retained audit found no unauthorized task-data access.</p>
<div class="source-links">{source('astra/prompt.txt','Astra prompt')}{source('sol/prompt.txt','Sol prompt')}{source('astra/access-audit.json','Astra access audit')}{source('sol/access-audit.json','Sol access audit')}{source('tdn-results-freeze.json','TDN freeze before results were opened')}</div>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Network</th><th>Architectural profile</th><th>Devices</th><th>Targets</th><th>Astra exact</th><th>Sol exact</th><th>TDN exact</th></tr></thead><tbody>{''.join(networkrows)}</tbody></table></div><p>The 20 requirement targets repeat across five architectural cases. The study contains 20 fault patterns and 100 network/requirement cases. Those cases are correlated and should not be treated as 100 independently sampled defect types.</p></section>
<section id="repairs"><h2>The preparation repairs restored usable protected tunnels</h2><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Established the existing connections</h3><p>The initial deployments had no active SAs after fault injection. The revised preparation reloads credentials and connections, establishes Outer connections first, and then establishes Inner connections. All <b>44 tunnel endpoints</b> had installed CHILD SAs before the agents started.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Repaired the duplicated certificate’s peer identity</h3><p>The deliberate certificate duplication remains a fault. Its peer now expects the duplicated identity, allowing authentication to complete. The repair prevents an unintended identity mismatch from hiding the certificate-uniqueness violation.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Replaced two disruptive fault types</h3><p>Transport-mode ESP combined with the existing subnet selectors prevented CHILD SA establishment. The revised profiles restore tunnel mode and replace IR-1/OR-1 targets with external GRE allowances under PF-5/PF-3. Each network still has 20 distinct target requirements.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Exposed the observations needed to assess the devices</h3><p>The generic gateway includes per-interface reverse-path settings and HTTP service content. The HTTP service explicitly serves synthetic user files, making its purpose observable. Neither capability supplies a requirement list.</p></article></div>
<p>The supervisor verified all 100 designated behaviors after injection. After both audits, it restored the clean configurations and reran the same 100 checks; none observed the planted behavior. The faulty configurations were then reapplied and checked again. The anti-spoofing ambiguity concerns the meaning of one observed acceptance, not whether the packet passed.</p>
<div class="source-links">{source('fault-design-revision.json','Changed fault targets')}{source('raw/revised-convergence.json','Pre-audit tunnel establishment')}{source('raw/oracle/corrected/net-01.json','Example faulty observations')}{source('raw/oracle/clean-repeat/net-01.json','Matching clean observations')}{link(BASE/'prepare_blind_networks.py','Preparation code')}</div></section>
<section id="lean"><h2>Lean rejected the faulty snapshots and accepted the clean snapshots</h2><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>5 / 5</strong><span>Faulty data-plane proof applications rejected</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>5 / 5</strong><span>Fresh clean data-plane proof applications passed</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>65</strong><span>Isolated local proof attempts rejected with actual Lean errors</span></div></div>
<p>The driver imports observed device records into Lean and applies <code>protected_red_stack_from_local_checks</code>, the existing general argument for double encryption at Black interfaces. The proof must first establish conditions on routes, links, switching, installed tunnel state, and firewall rules. The faulty snapshots fail the firewall condition.</p>
<div class="proof-chain"><div><b>Observed configuration and state</b><br>Routes, ordered rules, interfaces, links, OVS switching, and installed XFRM records become typed Lean values through <code>load_network_records</code>.</div><div class="failed"><b>Failed local condition: <code>TableDrivenForwardChecks</code></b><br>A captured allowance permits a transition that does not preserve the required encapsulation stack.</div><div><b>General proof remains unchanged</b><br><code>local_stack_conditions_of_finite_checks</code> supplies the premises used by <code>protected_red_stack_from_local_checks</code>.</div><div><b>The concrete snapshot theorem is blocked</b><br>The failed premise prevents accepting the double-encryption proof for that snapshot. The error does not prove that every path or the complete global requirement is violated.</div></div>
<p>The retained Lean diagnostic says:</p><div class="log">Tactic `native_decide` evaluated that the proposition\n  TableDrivenForwardChecks devices ifaces\nis false</div>
<p><code>RuleDiagnostics.lean</code> locates individual failing conditions. A separate <code>LocalCompliance.lean</code> invocation then attempts a positive proof of each condition and records Lean’s rejection. Existing protocol and multicast theorems are also applied to the captured INPUT, FORWARD, and OUTPUT chains as appropriate. The experiment uses no Python configuration-audit findings in its TDN score.</p>
<div class="note"><p><b>A concrete example:</b> <code>NB_I_A1</code> accepts UDP from the Red host to the Gray firewall on port 43110. The rule lacks an outbound IPsec-policy requirement, and the destination is outside the installed selectors. The imported rule fails the local stack condition. The corresponding live probe observes the packet leaving Gray; the clean control drops it.</p><p>The failure supports the local IR-3 violation. The result does not claim that the same packet later reaches Black or another Red enclave.</p></div>
<p>Restoring only the independently captured clean filters makes all five data-plane proofs pass. Fresh complete clean snapshots also pass. The first check isolates the changed rules; the second verifies an actual collected control configuration.</p>
<h3>The proof applications cover only part of the document</h3><p>The retained applications check the data-plane stack theorem and existing protocol/multicast conditions. They do <b>not instantiate all 24 selected RG specifications</b>. Forty planted cases concern algorithms, certificate settings, IKE versions/lifetimes, or spare interfaces that the project assigns to direct configuration checks. Those cases are excluded from the Lean detection count.</p><p>Another 25 configurations produce related Lean errors without establishing their specific target requirement. For example, the unlisted-peer and spoofed-source rules also bypass encryption; their failed stack conditions do not prove the peer or source-address restrictions. The Outer OSPF mutation also causes protocol and multicast guard failures, while the prohibition on running a routing process needs its own evidence binding. The five Outer Firewall HTTP-service targets are outside the instantiated checks.</p>
<h3>The importer repairs are part of the experiment record</h3><p>The first import rejected an isolated dummy interface and the optional management zone. The parser now retains both. It also derives local-interface labels from a device’s single declared zone, correcting labels on Gray routers and management-only switches. No general Lean proof or packet-processing relation changed. These implementation repairs and the initial failures are retained separately.</p>
<p><code>native_decide</code> uses native computation. The accepted trust assumptions include Lean’s compiler and native toolchain. The experiment does not describe those finite decisions as pure kernel reduction.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>TDN timing component</th><th>Measured time</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Native snapshot collection window</th><td>{collection_span/60:.2f} minutes</td><td>Five networks collected concurrently; processing supplements follow.</td></tr><tr><th>Faulty data-plane theorem applications</th><td>{proof_span:.2f} seconds wall time</td><td>Existing general proofs, warm dependencies, three jobs at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Localization and clean-filter replay</th><td>{local['runtime_seconds']/60:.2f} minutes</td><td>65 separately compiled failing conditions plus control checks.</td></tr><tr><th>Fresh clean theorem applications</th><td>{span_seconds(clean):.2f} seconds wall time</td><td>Five complete clean snapshots.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="footnote">Agent runtimes include reading, live queries, analysis, and output writing. The TDN timings separate collection from checking and exclude prior proof construction and supervisor implementation repairs. They do not support a direct end-to-end speed comparison.</p>
<div class="source-links">{link(BASE/'SnapshotCompliance.lean','Snapshot theorem application',24)}{link(ROOT/'TDN/Models/TableDrivenDataPlane.lean','Existing finite condition',94)}{link(ROOT/'TDN/Requirements/DoubleEncryptionOnBlackLinks.lean','Existing general proof',34)}{source('tdn/net-01/typecheck.jsonl','Actual faulty typecheck errors')}{source('clean-tdn/net-01/typecheck-result.json','Actual clean result')}{source('local-typechecks.json','All isolated checks')}{source('header-typechecks.json','Protocol and multicast theorem applications')}</div></section>
<section id="detectors"><h2>The agents read the requirements and compared them with device evidence</h2><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Astra High</h3><p>Astra extracted the PDF, read the architecture and requirement tables, and collected all advertised observation kinds. It checked routing, selector ranges, rule order, certificate identities, loaded connections, and active SAs. It grouped repeated device findings by requirement and network.</p><p>The audit used <b>{stats['astra']['device_observations']:,} device observations</b> and finished in <b>{stats['astra']['runtime_seconds']/60:.2f} minutes</b>. It explicitly withheld the redundant-topology anti-spoofing case and several claims that required organizational policy evidence.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Sol High</h3><p>Sol extracted the PDF, queried the network inventory, and collected configurations and operational state. It checked original table wording and corrected its quotations. It reported the GRE allowances under PF-11 and the unlisted destination under PF-20.</p><p>The audit used <b>{stats['sol']['device_observations']:,} device observations</b> and finished in <b>{stats['sol']['runtime_seconds']/60:.2f} minutes</b>. Four SSH multiplexing errors affected configuration reads; Sol retried all four successfully. It submitted no anti-spoofing finding.</p></article></div><p>Neither agent received feedback about missing cases during its audit. Both completed without a supervisor hint, a target count, or an imposed property list. Each ran once, so the experiment provides no estimate of run-to-run variability.</p><div class="source-links">{source('astra/output/findings.json','Astra response')}{source('sol/output/findings.json','Sol response')}{source('astra/runtime.json','Astra runtime')}{source('sol/runtime.json','Sol runtime')}</div></section>
<section id="requirements"><h2>Coverage differs by requirement type</h2><p>Each row contains five planted targets. Bold numbers count exact requirement matches; the smaller numbers include correct related findings about the same configuration.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Implanted change</th><th>Astra</th><th>Sol</th><th>TDN</th></tr></thead><tbody>{''.join(reqrows)}</tbody></table></div></section>
<section id="cases"><h2>Inspect each finding and its proof correspondence</h2><p>The evidence rows preserve the device, document requirement, Detector explanation, and actual Lean error where one exists.</p><div class="controls"><label>Search cases<input id="search" placeholder="Requirement, device, or change"></label><label>Network<select id="network"><option value="">All networks</option>{''.join(f'<option>net-{i:02}</option>' for i in range(1,6))}</select></label><label>Coverage<select id="coverage"><option value="">All cases</option><option value="missed">No exact TDN match</option></select></label></div><p id="count" class="footnote">100 cases</p><div class="table-wrap"><table id="case-table"><thead><tr><th>Network and device</th><th>Target</th><th>Astra</th><th>Sol</th><th>TDN</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody>{''.join(caserows)}</tbody></table></div></section>
<section id="limits"><h2>The results support a narrow comparison with explicit limits</h2><ul><li><b>Ground truth:</b> 100 behaviors were intentionally introduced and observed. One requirement interpretation remains unresolved. Both nominal and sensitivity scores are retained.</li><li><b>Coverage:</b> the Detectors searched the whole supplied document; the TDN run applied a bounded set of existing proofs. TDN’s lower score measures those instantiated checks, not every property that could be formalized.</li><li><b>Proof rejection:</b> an unproved sufficient condition is not automatically a counterexample to the global theorem. The correspondence uses the exact rule, clean controls, and independent live observations.</li><li><b>Model gap:</b> Lean checks the imported model. The experiment does not verify Linux, strongSwan, the parsers, or the abstract packet semantics end to end.</li><li><b>Repeated patterns:</b> the same 20 target types recur across five networks. One result per model cannot establish a general ranking of model reliability.</li><li><b>Network stability:</b> collection checks selected before/after fields. It does not prove atomicity or correctness during every routing transition.</li><li><b>Blinding:</b> command access was enforced by the gateway. Filesystem blindness was instructed and audited, with no observed violation; it was not enforced by a separate machine or OS sandbox.</li></ul><p>The strongest verified result is the paired behavior of the actual proof applications: all five faulty data-plane snapshots are rejected, while all five fresh clean snapshots pass. The detailed requirement scores explain how much of the planted defect set those applications address.</p></section>
<section id="records"><h2>The experiment can be inspected and replayed from its retained records</h2><p>The new report and run are separate from earlier experiments. The run stores original and revised profiles, clean and faulty snapshots, detector prompts and responses, command-access logs, Lean sources and diagnostics, source hashes, adjudication, and validation results.</p><div class="source-links">{source('scoring.json','Scores and every case mapping')}{source('validation.json','Citation, input, and proof validation')}{source('injection-freeze.json','Fault-profile freeze')}{source('execution-import-final.json','Final import results')}{link(BASE/'README-blind-theory.md','Replay and scope documentation')}{link(BASE/'score_blind_theory.py','Scoring rules')}{link(BASE/'build_blind_report.py','Report generator')}</div><p>The report follows the project’s existing light Lavish design. It embeds its content, styling, and interaction code in one HTML file. Nothing was committed, pushed, or published to the website.</p></section><footer>TDN v3.3 | Local experiment report | Source run: 20261112-blind-theory-01</footer></main></div>
<script>
document.getElementById('theme').onclick=()=>{{const dark=document.documentElement.dataset.theme!=='dark';document.documentElement.dataset.theme=dark?'dark':'light';document.getElementById('theme').textContent=dark?'Light theme':'Dark theme'}};
for(const b of document.querySelectorAll('[data-detail]'))b.onclick=()=>{{const r=document.getElementById(b.dataset.detail);r.hidden=!r.hidden;b.setAttribute('aria-expanded',String(!r.hidden));}};
function filter(){{const q=document.getElementById('search').value.toLowerCase(),n=document.getElementById('network').value,c=document.getElementById('coverage').value;let total=0;for(const row of document.querySelectorAll('tr.case')){{const visible=row.dataset.search.includes(q)&&(!n||row.dataset.network===n)&&(!c||row.dataset.missed==='true');row.hidden=!visible;const d=row.nextElementSibling;if(!visible)d.hidden=true;total+=visible?1:0;}}document.getElementById('count').textContent=total+' cases shown';}}
for(const id of ['search','network','coverage'])document.getElementById(id).addEventListener('input',filter);
</script></body></html>'''
path=ROOT/'.lavish/blind-theory-comparison.html';path.write_text(content)
print(json.dumps({'report':str(path),'bytes':path.stat().st_size,'cases':len(score['cases'])}))
